    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm having a nightmare with our jenkins pipeline. the unit test stage is being super flaky. it fails about 20% of the time but the codebase hasn't changed and the tests pass if i just retry the build. i've checked the basic stuff but i can't figure out what's causing these non-deterministic failures. any ideas on how to isolate the environment or what might be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the dreaded flaky tests! It's like a mischievous goblin playing pranks on your pipeline. First, let's try to isolate the environment. You could create a separate Jenkins slave or agent that mimics your production environment as closely as possible. This way, you can see if the flaky behavior persists in a controlled setting. \n\nAnother approach is to introduce some randomness into your tests. Sometimes, the environment can be the culprit, like a mischievous goblin hiding in the shadows. By adding randomness, you might be able to catch the goblin in the act. For instance, you could randomize the order of test execution or introduce delays between tests. \n\nLastly, consider using a tool like Chaos Monkey to introduce controlled chaos into your tests. It's like inviting a goblin to your party and seeing how well your tests can handle the unexpected. This might help you identify the root cause of the flaky behavior. Good luck, and may the goblin be ever in your favor!",
      "source": "target",
